issue.body:
## Rust reproducer

```rust
trait T<Arg> {
    fn f(arg: Arg);
}

impl T<&mut u8> for u16 {
    fn f(arg: &mut u8) {

    }
}
```

## Problem

The extracted lean looks like:
```lean
structure T (Self : Type) (Arg : Type) where
  f : Arg → Result Unit

def U16.Insts.Aeneas_testTMut0U8.f (arg : Std.U8) : Result Std.U8 := do
  ok arg

def U16.Insts.Aeneas_testTMut0U8 : T Std.U16 Std.U8 := {
  f := U16.Insts.Aeneas_testTMut0U8.f
}
```
Which is invalid because the expected signature for `f` is to return `Result Unit` and it is instantiated with `Result Std.U8`

It is quite similar to https://github.com/AeneasVerif/aeneas/issues/960 as both issues come from trait definitions which make assumptions on type parameters and associated types, and impls that don't verify these assumptions. 

I found these issues when trying to write code with closures taking `&mut` arguments.

comments.body:
I have hit the same issue with this independent minimization:

```rust
fn f(s: &mut [u32]) {
    let _ = s.iter_mut().take(1);
}
```

**Versions**:
- Aeneas: daa85d7e8940 (i.e. latest as of the time of submission)
- Charon: 0.1.232

For completeness, here's an example with associated types:

```rust
trait Foo {
    type Bar;
    fn bar(self) -> Self::Bar;
}
impl<'a> Foo for &'a mut u8 {
    type Bar = &'a mut u8;
    fn bar(self) -> &'a mut u8 {
        self
    }
}

fn foo() {
    let mut x = 42u8;
    let y = x.bar();
}
```

```
error: Foo.lean:32:9: Type mismatch
  FooFooMutAU8.bar
has type
  U8 → Result (U8 × (U8 → U8))
but is expected to have type
  U8 → Result U8
```

Note that there's 2 issues: one due to `Self` as an argument and one due to the associated type as a result.

pull_request.body:
The cases below were not supported.

The symbolic evaluation forgot to introduce a region abstraction to project the 'static lifetime of `Y` when introducing the corresponding symbolic value:
```rust
pub struct S(pub &'static u8);

const Y: S = S(&0);

pub fn f() -> S {
    Y
}
```

This is due to a limitation when merging shared borrows in joins - the temporary solution is to detect the case and make it recoverable in the join:
```rust
pub struct M(pub &'static &'static u8);

pub fn f(_m: M, a: bool) {
    if a {}
    if a {}
}
```
